# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text vs binary. `eol=lf` pins the working tree to
# LF regardless of platform or `core.autocrlf`.
#
# This is set as the default rather than per extension because the per-extension
# version had a hole in it: files with no covered extension - `.gitignore`,
# `.toml`, future dotfiles - fell back to `text=auto` alone, so with
# core.autocrlf=true (the Git default on Windows) they checked out as CRLF. Then
# any tool that rewrote them, and every `git diff` touching them, produced a
# whole-file diff for a one-line change. Three files were already in that state.
#
# `eol=lf` also makes the working tree agree with Prettier's `endOfLine: lf`.
# Without it, `format:check` passed on a freshly formatted tree and then failed
# on every source file after the next checkout, for a line ending nobody chose.
* text=auto eol=lf

# SQL migrations: LF always. These are applied to Postgres through tooling that
# reads bytes; a stray CRLF inside a dollar-quoted function body is a real
# hazard, and these files are the financial record's definition.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or they fail on Unix with "bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are generated; leave them alone entirely.
package-lock.json -text
